      limits: "<p>What is counted is pages that hold a word of wrongdoing (illegal levies, extortion, assault, beating, molestation, rape, fraud, bribery, corruption, drugs, shooting, harassment, and their verb forms) and name a group; the word list is in the question file. Sharing a page does not mean “oknum” is said of that group: police, prosecutors and judges are often on such pages as the ones handling a case, so their figures are too low. The cleaner comparison is teachers, civil servants and soldiers against legislators and doctors. A page naming two groups counts for both. Of the 200 places we read, 40 are ordinary people with no office (“oknum pasien”, “oknum masyarakat”) and 12 are unclear; two thirds of that sample is from Tribunnews, because places were drawn in proportion to pages. This does not say why: people of low rank are more often left unnamed, and the word “oknum” fills the place of the name.</p><p><b>The word after “oknum”.</b> For the second figure we first counted, in a third of the text, every word standing right after “oknum”, then grouped the seventy most frequent into seventeen rows and counted them all in the whole text. One page can fall in several rows. At least 15–29% of “oknum” pages, depending on the publisher, fall in no row.</p>",
